* verify-txn.bp - the BASIC half of gplbld/verify-txn.py.  PORT_ADOPTION
* queue 22; the intent is the port's gplbld/verify-txn.ps1.
*
* IT IS STAGED, COMPILED AND RUN BY verify-txn.py IN THE CALLER'S OWN ACCOUNT.
* It is an ordinary program - NOT $internal - so it compiles as an unprivileged
* user with no developer build.
*
* ***EVERY ANSWER IS PRINTED AS "TAG=value" ON ITS OWN LINE, AND EACH TAG IS
* PRINTED ON EXACTLY ONE PATH.***  The Python half anchors on the tag, so a
* refusal, a missing file or a fault cannot be mistaken for an answer: the tag
* simply never appears, and the row fails.  Nothing here prints a bare "OK".
*
* ***IT LEAVES ITS FIXTURES BEHIND ON PURPOSE.***  The second instrument is the
* Python half looking at what is actually ON DISK, and a probe that tidied up
* would destroy the evidence before it could be read.  verify-txn.py removes
* them after it has looked.
*
program zztxn

$include syscom keys.h

   open 'zztxnd' to dirf else
      crt 'ZZTXN.FATAL=cannot open zztxnd'
      stop
   end

   crt 'ZZTXN.BEGIN=1'

* ------------------------------------------------------------------ section 1
* DOES COMMIT END THE TRANSACTION IT COMMITS?  The port's headline defect:
* op_txnbgn() incremented the level and op_txncmt() never decremented it, so
* SYSTEM(1008) climbed for ever and a program could not ask "am I in a
* transaction".  L2 is the whole check; L0 and L1 are what make L2 mean
* something rather than being a zero that was never anything else.

* ***A WRITE INSIDE A TRANSACTION NEEDS THE LOCK ALREADY HELD, AND READU TAKES
* IT EVEN FOR A RECORD THAT DOES NOT EXIST YET.***  Measured 12 Sep 2026, not
* read: the first version of this probe wrote without locking and got 3023,
* "no lock is held on it.  Nothing was written." - which is the improved
* wording this tree ships, doing its job.  Every write and the delete below
* therefore take READU first.

   crt 'L0=':system(SYS$TRANS_LEVEL)

   begin transaction
      crt 'L1=':system(SYS$TRANS_LEVEL)
      readu junk from dirf, 'S1' else junk = ''
      write 'one' to dirf, 'S1'
      commit
   end transaction

   crt 'L2=':system(SYS$TRANS_LEVEL)

   read r from dirf, 'S1' then crt 'S1=':r else crt 'S1=NOTFOUND'

* ------------------------------------------------------------------ section 2
* A NESTED COMMIT MUST NOT LOSE THE OUTER TRANSACTION'S WRITES.  This is the
* one the port says the verifier exists for: a nested commit zeroed
* process.txn_id while leaving the OUTER transaction's cache orphaned, so the
* outer COMMIT committed an EMPTY cache and its writes vanished WITH NO
* MESSAGE.  Part of a transaction landing and part not is the single outcome a
* transaction exists to prevent, and nothing reported it.
*
* S2 is written by the OUTER transaction BEFORE the inner one runs, so it is
* exactly the write that went missing.

* ***AND THE TRANSACTION NUMBER, WHICH IS THE THIRD OF A4's THREE PARTS AND THE
* LAST TO BE MEASURED (12 Sep 2026).***  SYSTEM(1007) is the transaction NUMBER,
* zero if none - a different question from the LEVEL.  After the inner COMMIT
* the session is back inside the OUTER transaction, so 1007 must name the OUTER
* one again: N3 must equal N1.  A level that came back to 1 while the number
* was left on the inner transaction (or on zero) would mean end_txn_level()
* decremented the counter without reinstating the parent, which is precisely
* what A4 lifted out of rollback() and into end_txn_level().
*
* ***THE NUMBERS ARE NOT LITERALS AND MUST NOT BE ASSERTED AS ANY.***  They are
* allocated per transaction and differ every run, so the checks are
* RELATIONSHIPS - N1 non-zero, N3 = N1, N5 = 0.  N2 is printed as context.

   begin transaction
      crt 'N1=':system(SYS$TRANS_NBR)
      readu junk from dirf, 'S2' else junk = ''
      write 'two' to dirf, 'S2'

      begin transaction
         crt 'N2=':system(SYS$TRANS_NBR)
         readu junk from dirf, 'S3' else junk = ''
         write 'three' to dirf, 'S3'
         commit
      end transaction

      crt 'L3=':system(SYS$TRANS_LEVEL)
      crt 'N3=':system(SYS$TRANS_NBR)
      commit
   end transaction

   crt 'L4=':system(SYS$TRANS_LEVEL)
   crt 'N5=':system(SYS$TRANS_NBR)

   read r from dirf, 'S2' then crt 'S2=':r else crt 'S2=NOTFOUND'
   read r from dirf, 'S3' then crt 'S3=':r else crt 'S3=NOTFOUND'

* ------------------------------------------------------------------ section 3
* A DIRECTORY FILE'S RECORD ID BECOMES A FILENAME, AND HAS TO BE MAPPED.
* map_dir_ids turns each of "*,=><%/+:;?\" into "%" plus a letter
* (sd.h:113-114), so the id "," is the file "%C".  op_txncmt() handed
* dir_write() and the delete path the RAW id, so a WRITE inside a transaction
* created a file the matching READ could never find, and a DELETE removed a
* path that had never existed, tolerated the ENOENT and reported success.
*
* ***ON LINUX EVERY ONE OF THOSE THIRTEEN CHARACTERS IS A LEGAL FILENAME
* CHARACTER, WHICH MAKES THIS WORSE HERE THAN IN THE PORT.***  The port chose
* ids that are legal Windows filenames in BOTH forms so that the failure would
* be silent rather than loud; here there is no other kind - an unmapped write
* always succeeds, under the wrong name.
*
* CTL is written OUTSIDE a transaction and is the control: if it were absent
* too, the section would be measuring directory files in general rather than
* transactions.  DEL is created outside and removed inside, because a delete
* can only be tested on something that was there.

   write 'ctl' to dirf, '='
   write 'del' to dirf, ';'

   begin transaction
      readu junk from dirf, ',' else junk = ''
      write 'wrt' to dirf, ','
      readu junk from dirf, ';' else junk = ''
      delete dirf, ';'
      commit
   end transaction

   crt 'L5=':system(SYS$TRANS_LEVEL)

   read r from dirf, '=' then crt 'CTL=':r else crt 'CTL=NOTFOUND'
   read r from dirf, ',' then crt 'WRT=':r else crt 'WRT=NOTFOUND'
   read r from dirf, ';' then crt 'DEL=':r else crt 'DEL=NOTFOUND'

   crt 'ZZTXN.END=1'

end
